  The plugin descriptor.

  A note on punctuation before anything else, because this file has already broken the build
  once for it: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, and the IntelliJ Platform Gradle
  plugin's patchPluginXml task fails the whole build with
  "org.jdom2.input.JDOMParseException: Error on line 12: The string is not permitted within
  comments" rather than skipping the comment. The rest of this directory uses a double hyphen
  the way prose uses a dash. Do not carry that habit into this file.
  packaging/chocolatey/ash.nuspec carries the same warning for the same reason.

  NO version AND NO idea-version ELEMENT HERE, ON PURPOSE

  Both are written into this file by patchPluginXml, from `version` and
  `pluginConfiguration.ideaVersion` in build.gradle.kts. Declaring them here as well would
  mean two sources of truth for a number, and the one in this file would be the stale one,
  because patchPluginXml replaces what it finds.

  That also keeps this file out of pyproject.toml's [tool.commitizen] version_files, and
  build.gradle.kts explains at length why the plugin version does not track ASH's at all.

  WHY THERE IS NO depends ON A LANGUAGE PLUGIN

  The inspection is registered without a language attribute, so it runs for every file type.
  ASH scans a repository and reports findings in whatever it finds: Python, TypeScript,
  Terraform, CloudFormation YAML, Dockerfiles, Ruby. Restricting the registration to one
  language would silently drop findings in every other file type. com.intellij.modules.platform
  is the only dependency, which is what makes this plugin installable in every JetBrains IDE
  rather than only in IDEA.
-->
<idea-plugin>
    <id>io.github.awslabs.ash</id>
    <name>ASH - Automated Security Helper</name>
    <vendor url="https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper">Amazon Web Services</vendor>

    <description><![CDATA[
    Runs the <a href="https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper">Automated
    Security Helper</a> (ASH) command line tool over the open project and shows the findings
    inline, at the file and line the scanner reported.
    <br/><br/>
    This plugin contains no scanners and no copy of ASH. It invokes the <code>ashx</code>
    command on your PATH, falls back to <code>ash</code> when <code>ashx</code> is not
    installed, or runs the executable set in <b>Settings | Tools | ASH</b>, and reads the
    SARIF report ASH writes.
    <br/><br/>
    <b>Usage:</b> <i>Tools | Run ASH Security Scan</i>. Findings appear as editor errors and
    warnings, with severity taken from the SARIF report. A scan ASH reports as incomplete
    still shows the findings it produced, and says which scanners did not run.
    ]]></description>

    <depends>com.intellij.modules.platform</depends>

    <extensions defaultExtensionNs="com.intellij">
        <!-- Application level, because the path to a binary is a property of the machine. -->
        <applicationService serviceImplementation="io.github.awslabs.ash.jetbrains.AshSettings"/>

        <applicationConfigurable
                parentId="tools"
                instance="io.github.awslabs.ash.jetbrains.AshSettingsConfigurable"
                id="io.github.awslabs.ash.settings"
                displayName="ASH"/>

        <!-- No `language` attribute: the inspection then runs for every file type, which
             is what ASH scanning a whole repository requires. Naming a language here
             would silently skip everything not named. -->
        <localInspection
                implementationClass="io.github.awslabs.ash.jetbrains.AshFindingInspection"
                shortName="AshFinding"
                displayName="ASH security finding"
                groupName="Security"
                enabledByDefault="true"
                level="WARNING"/>

        <notificationGroup
                id="ASH"
                displayType="BALLOON"/>
    </extensions>

    <actions>
        <action
                id="io.github.awslabs.ash.jetbrains.AshScanAction"
                class="io.github.awslabs.ash.jetbrains.AshScanAction"
                text="Run ASH Security Scan"
                description="Run the installed ASH CLI over this project and show findings inline">
            <!-- ToolsMenu only. An AnalyzeMenu entry was tried and removed: that group
                 is not registered in IntelliJ IDEA Community, and the platform logs
                 "group with id AnalyzeMenu isn't registered so the action won't be added
                 to it" at error level on every startup. The action stays reachable from
                 Tools and from Find Action. -->
            <add-to-group group-id="ToolsMenu" anchor="last"/>
        </action>
    </actions>
</idea-plugin>
